1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,920
It was a war in which brother fought brother, where battlefields became

2
00:00:04,921 --> 00:00:09,840
slaughterhouses, where the technology and ingenuity of an industrial revolution

3
00:00:09,841 --> 00:00:13,800
were put to the task of making the most destructive killing machines the world

4
00:00:13,801 --> 00:00:19,560
had ever seen. From the Springfield to the Spencer, from the Napoleon to the

5
00:00:19,561 --> 00:00:25,800
Gatling gun, these were the first truly modern weapons. Now, the guns of the Civil

6
00:00:25,801 --> 00:00:28,040
War on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:42,260 --> 00:00:45,820
When the American Civil War began in 1861,

8
00:00:45,821 --> 00:00:48,760
no one imagined that four years of bloodshed and suffering

9
00:00:48,761 --> 00:00:49,461
lay ahead.

10
00:00:55,800 --> 00:01:02,800
Gun manufacturing and design had improved dramatically since the American Revolution.

11
00:01:02,801 --> 00:01:10,800
Yet there was little attempt to revise existing battlefield tactics to keep pace with new technological advances in weapons.

12
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:21,800
Throughout the war, field commanders on both sides used outmoded strategies that doomed their men to certain death.

13
00:01:21,801 --> 00:01:22,501
in depth.

14
00:01:22,502 --> 00:01:27,540
Troops were lined up shoulder to shoulder and marched in a deadly frontal assault

15
00:01:27,541 --> 00:01:30,140
directly into a wall of musket fire.

16
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:41,060
Only through sheer numbers, it was thought, could a defended position be taken.

17
00:01:42,620 --> 00:01:49,820
The tactics that were used are generally called Napoleonic tactics, which are linear tactics.

18
00:01:49,821 --> 00:02:02,280
The earlier weapons, the smoothbore weapons, the weapons that were inaccurate at 100 yards on individual targets, were the basic weapons of Napoleonic tactics.

19
00:02:02,940 --> 00:02:09,940
The manufacture of infantry weapons had come a long way since the time of Napoleon, particularly the musket.

20
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:19,560
In 1855, the Springfield Armory in Massachusetts perfected the Springfield Rifled Musket, the most widely used gun in the Civil War.

21
00:02:19,820 --> 00:02:27,760
Rifling, or the machining of spiral grooves in the barrel, gave projectiles stability and dramatically increased accuracy.

22
00:02:28,660 --> 00:02:39,540
The tactical manuals did not modernize their text when the rifle musket was adopted in 1855.

23
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:43,900
They were using outmoded tactics with modern weapons.

24
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:49,340
The enlisted man who was on the firing line figured it out quickly.

25
00:02:49,820 --> 00:02:56,640
that the weapons they were using were killing at 300 to 500 yards instead of the old 80 to 100 yards.

26
00:02:57,580 --> 00:03:01,920
Almost a million Springfields were utilized by both sides in the Civil War.

27
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:07,220
They were reliable, rugged, simple in construction, and finely engineered.

28
00:03:08,080 --> 00:03:12,120
The Springfield, along with the similarly modeled Enfield, a British import,

29
00:03:12,121 --> 00:03:17,120
were the guns responsible for more deaths than any other weapon during the Civil War.

30
00:03:19,820 --> 00:03:28,460
Originally produced at a cost of $13.93 per gun, a Springfield in good condition can now bring $2,000 at auction.

31
00:03:30,360 --> 00:03:33,960
Among its innovations was the interchangeability of parts.

32
00:03:34,940 --> 00:03:42,580
With the machinery capable of producing firearms made from interchangeable parts, you could produce a lot more of them a lot quicker.

33
00:03:42,581 --> 00:03:49,740
If during a battle you broke a piece off of your gun, say the hammer or the mainspring,

34
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:54,880
you could pretty much pick a mainspring off of another gun and with maybe some minor fitting,

35
00:03:55,400 --> 00:04:00,720
interchange that broken part with the part that you needed to make your gun function again.

36
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:05,780
Another technological innovation around the time of the Civil War

37
00:04:05,781 --> 00:04:10,820
was a new and highly destructive projectile made for the musket, the mini-ball.

38
00:04:10,821 --> 00:04:17,820
It was developed in France in 1849 by Captain Manet of the French Army.

39
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:25,200
The mini ball itself is a conical-shaped projectile,

40
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:31,420
whereas most of the firearms up to that time had incorporated a round lead ball.

41
00:04:32,260 --> 00:04:38,280
A simple yet revolutionary design, the mini ball was utilized by both sides during the war

42
00:04:38,281 --> 00:04:42,340
and was probably the single greatest cause of battlefield casualties.

43
00:04:43,260 --> 00:04:49,280
The wounds that were created by a slow-moving large projectile

44
00:04:49,281 --> 00:04:53,160
that was relatively soft by nature were horrific.

45
00:04:54,420 --> 00:05:01,340
The mini-ball, upon penetration, it would expand because it was soft-lead.

46
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:06,320
Because of its low velocity, it had a tendency to wander

47
00:05:06,321 --> 00:05:13,000
If it impacted bone, instead of breaking through the bone, it would shatter the bone and travel up the bone.

48
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:18,660
It was hard to trace the trajectory once it entered the body.

49
00:05:19,340 --> 00:05:27,100
Because of the size, because of the low velocity, it had a tendency to inflict very horrible wounds.

50
00:05:27,101 --> 00:05:37,600
the mini ball was a godsend for the soldier in the field because its slightly smaller diameter

51
00:05:37,601 --> 00:05:45,480
allowed for easier loading of the musket but for a wounded soldier the mini ball often proved to be

52
00:05:45,481 --> 00:05:53,860
his worst nightmare medical science was so far behind military science that it was a tragedy

53
00:05:53,861 --> 00:06:00,100
Medical science had no idea of infection, had no idea of sterilization.

54
00:06:01,900 --> 00:06:04,020
Probing was done with a dirty finger.

55
00:06:05,140 --> 00:06:12,160
When an extremity was hit, if it was shattered, an arm or a leg, the simplest solution was immediate amputation,

56
00:06:12,780 --> 00:06:19,500
which automatically gave the poor soldier an infection that he would rarely survive if he even survived the shock.

57
00:06:19,501 --> 00:06:31,981
If a soldier was hit in the body or the head, he was usually laid aside because the surgeons felt that there was no point in wasting time on a wound that would be

58
00:06:31,982 --> 00:06:33,060
surely mortal.

59
00:06:35,440 --> 00:06:41,400
The horrors inflicted by a more effective combination of rifled musket and the miniball were shocking.

60
00:06:42,180 --> 00:06:45,642
For hundreds of thousands of soldiers like Colonel T.D.

61
00:06:45,643 --> 00:06:48,600
Kingsley, the carnage was almost beyond belief.

62
00:06:49,500 --> 00:06:53,480
I never wished to see another such time as the 27th of May.

63
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:57,760
The doctors used a large cotton press for the butchering room,

64
00:06:58,300 --> 00:07:00,420
and when I was carried into the building and looked about,

65
00:07:00,820 --> 00:07:03,760
I could not help comparing the surgeons to fiends.

66
00:07:04,600 --> 00:07:06,600
All around the ground lay the wounded men.

67
00:07:07,460 --> 00:07:12,060
Some of them were shrieking, some cursing and swearing, and some praying.

68
00:07:13,060 --> 00:07:15,780
In the middle of the room were dissecting tables,

69
00:07:16,020 --> 00:07:18,140
and they were covered with blood all over them.

70
00:07:18,141 --> 00:07:23,840
and by the sides of the tables was a heap of feet, legs, and arms.

71
00:07:24,920 --> 00:07:27,100
T.D. Kingsley, 1863.

72
00:07:29,280 --> 00:07:32,320
But the mini ball and the Springfield musket that fired it

73
00:07:32,321 --> 00:07:34,580
were by no means the ultimate weapons.

74
00:07:36,420 --> 00:07:38,620
Loading the musket was a cumbersome process

75
00:07:38,621 --> 00:07:42,420
that allowed a soldier to fire only two to three rounds per minute.

76
00:07:42,421 --> 00:07:52,660
The soldier in battle would take a cartridge from his cartridge box and prepare to load the gun by biting off the end of the cartridge.

77
00:07:53,360 --> 00:08:01,600
He would tear off the cartridge, pour the powder down the muzzle, seat the mini ball, draw his rammer,

78
00:08:02,400 --> 00:08:06,440
and then forcing the round and the powder down to the breech of the gun.

79
00:08:06,441 --> 00:08:10,840
and then raise the gun up again to chest height,

80
00:08:11,440 --> 00:08:14,240
remove a percussion cap from the side pouch,

81
00:08:14,680 --> 00:08:16,520
place the percussion cap on the cone,

82
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:19,360
and then they were to return the shoulder arms.

83
00:08:19,520 --> 00:08:20,840
They were ready to commence fire.

84
00:08:24,360 --> 00:08:27,060
The entire process took some nine steps

85
00:08:27,061 --> 00:08:30,520
and was especially difficult for the soldier caught in a prone position.

86
00:08:31,060 --> 00:08:34,220
If any one part of the procedure was performed incorrectly,

87
00:08:34,780 --> 00:08:36,000
the gun would not fire.

88
00:08:36,440 --> 00:08:41,440
The Battle of Gettysburg dramatized this in a striking way.

89
00:08:43,160 --> 00:08:49,600
Of the 37,000 muskets salvaged after the battle, 24,000 were improperly loaded.

90
00:08:51,100 --> 00:08:55,440
During battlefield situations, soldiers sometimes became so frightened

91
00:08:55,441 --> 00:09:01,020
that they would pour powder and ball down the muzzle, ram it home,

92
00:09:01,620 --> 00:09:04,240
and then forget to cap and shoot the gun.

93
00:09:04,241 --> 00:09:08,300
after Gettysburg, tens of thousands of guns were found

94
00:09:08,301 --> 00:09:11,360
that had more than one load down the muzzle.

95
00:09:11,900 --> 00:09:15,180
And after a while, when you put in a certain number of charges,

96
00:09:15,380 --> 00:09:19,040
if you do cap it and pull the trigger, the gun's going to explode.

97
00:09:22,240 --> 00:09:25,760
The American Civil War was the final glory of the musket.

98
00:09:26,300 --> 00:09:29,940
The times demanded a more reliable weapon for the field soldier.

99
00:09:29,941 --> 00:09:34,440
It was a challenge the industry of the North was eagerly preparing to meet.

100
00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:38,500
A new kind of repeating rifle was already on the horizon.

101
00:09:39,880 --> 00:09:45,920
The country's first arsenal, the Springfield Armory, was founded by George Washington during the Revolutionary War

102
00:09:45,921 --> 00:09:48,780
and officially designated in 1794.

103
00:09:49,460 --> 00:09:52,720
The guns of the Civil War will continue on Modern Marvels.

104
00:09:54,340 --> 00:09:59,640
To the Civil War soldier, the musket, though effective, had obvious limitations.

105
00:09:59,940 --> 00:10:05,520
There was urgent need for a repeating weapon that could fire several bullets in rapid succession.

106
00:10:06,240 --> 00:10:11,680
But such a weapon had only become feasible with the perfection of a reliable, self-contained cartridge.

107
00:10:14,200 --> 00:10:19,520
Early cartridges had been unstable, susceptible to moisture, and often malfunctioned.

108
00:10:21,020 --> 00:10:24,940
Finally, a copper case was developed to house the fulminate in powder.

109
00:10:25,780 --> 00:10:27,600
A bullet was seated on top.

110
00:10:27,601 --> 00:10:33,600
By 1861, the self-contained cartridge had been a working reality for several years.

111
00:10:35,120 --> 00:10:40,840
Breach-loading firearms had been developed in the years prior to the Civil War,

112
00:10:41,380 --> 00:10:45,360
and with the development of the metallic or the self-contained cartridge,

113
00:10:45,760 --> 00:10:52,740
offered the soldier an opportunity to load and fire firearms with much greater rapidity.

114
00:10:53,540 --> 00:10:57,080
By late 1861, with the war in full swing,

115
00:10:57,600 --> 00:11:03,420
The race was on to develop a repeating rifle that could utilize this new self-contained cartridge technology.

116
00:11:04,500 --> 00:11:12,500
This is the Henry rifle, one of the most important of repeating firearms made in the Civil War period.

117
00:11:12,501 --> 00:11:16,640
Even though only about 1,700 were used by U.S.

118
00:11:16,641 --> 00:11:24,380
ordnance and issued to troops, many thousands were bought and issued by militia units.

119
00:11:24,381 --> 00:11:32,840
manufactured for the union by oliver winchester the henry's rapid fire capability so impressed

120
00:11:32,841 --> 00:11:37,940
soldiers that they nicknamed it the gun you loaded on sunday and shot all week

121
00:11:37,941 --> 00:11:47,140
the henry rifle with its multi-shot magazine and rapid firing lever allowed the trooper to fire

122
00:11:47,141 --> 00:11:50,920
as many as 60 shots in a minute.

123
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:56,740
So the Henry rifle was one of the most important innovations of the war.

124
00:11:57,520 --> 00:12:00,240
A direct precursor to the Winchester rifle,

125
00:12:00,600 --> 00:12:02,920
the Henry was originally sold to the U.S. Army

126
00:12:02,921 --> 00:12:05,500
for the then exorbitant price of $40.

127
00:12:08,500 --> 00:12:12,840
An original Henry can now command a sum of over $25,000.

128
00:12:14,640 --> 00:12:16,220
Despite his best efforts,

129
00:12:16,221 --> 00:12:20,740
Winchester was unable to supply the Henry to Union troops in sufficient quantities.

130
00:12:22,040 --> 00:12:25,340
Both the intricately designed gun and its ammunition

131
00:12:25,341 --> 00:12:27,960
proved too difficult to assemble in large numbers.

132
00:12:29,620 --> 00:12:33,400
But another manufacturer from Connecticut, Christopher Spencer,

133
00:12:33,740 --> 00:12:38,780
overcame those problems and created his own breech-loading repeater called the Spencer.

134
00:12:39,680 --> 00:12:41,520
Stronger and simpler in construction,

135
00:12:41,521 --> 00:12:46,340
This weapon was nicknamed the Horizontal Shot Tower by stunned Confederate troops,

136
00:12:46,680 --> 00:12:48,800
unlucky enough to come under its direct fire.

137
00:12:50,540 --> 00:12:54,680
Spencer, developed by Christopher Minor Spencer,

138
00:12:55,400 --> 00:12:59,360
had a seven-shot tube magazine in the breech

139
00:12:59,361 --> 00:13:06,880
that allowed it to be fired seven times by the operating of a lever underneath the weapon

140
00:13:06,881 --> 00:13:08,960
and the cocking of a hammer.

141
00:13:08,961 --> 00:13:15,840
By dropping the lever, you extracted the fired round and automatically loaded a new round.

142
00:13:16,340 --> 00:13:18,920
You cocked the hammer and fired the weapon.

143
00:13:19,700 --> 00:13:26,200
It meant that this weapon could be fired as rapidly as it could be operated,

144
00:13:26,400 --> 00:13:30,300
which gave the individual with this weapon a terrific advantage.

145
00:13:31,280 --> 00:13:34,840
The Spencer was an immediate success with Union soldiers in the field.

146
00:13:34,841 --> 00:13:40,840
Before long, exuberant testimonials were pouring into the company offices.

147
00:13:40,841 --> 00:13:45,840
It is admitted by all who have witnessed our practice that we have the best gun in the Army.

148
00:13:45,841 --> 00:13:48,840
On long ranges, they exceed our expectations.

149
00:13:48,841 --> 00:13:51,840
Captain G.M. Barber, Ohio sharpshooters.

150
00:13:51,841 --> 00:13:58,840
I am firmly of the opinion that 1,500 men armed with the Spencer Carbine

151
00:13:58,841 --> 00:14:03,840
are more than a match for 2,500 armed with any other firearm.

152
00:14:04,840 --> 00:14:07,580
George Armstrong Custer, Brigadier General.

153
00:14:09,420 --> 00:14:14,140
By 1863, the Spencer was fast becoming the weapon of choice.

154
00:14:14,440 --> 00:14:20,180
But its unique use of self-contained cartridges also gave the Spencer another unexpected quality,

155
00:14:20,181 --> 00:14:22,860
which was immensely valuable to Union forces.

156
00:14:25,160 --> 00:14:32,640
The Confederate forces captured a large number of Spencer rifles and carbines during the course of the war,

157
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:34,500
particularly in 1864.

158
00:14:34,840 --> 00:14:39,840
And Confederate ordnance facilities tried to manufacture Spencer cartridges,

159
00:14:40,120 --> 00:14:45,000
but they never had the technical capability to spin a copper case

160
00:14:45,001 --> 00:14:47,760
and therefore could never make the ammunition.

161
00:14:48,480 --> 00:14:53,680
And the Confederates, who were lucky enough to pick up a Spencer on the battlefield,

162
00:14:54,500 --> 00:15:00,180
only had the use of it as long as they could capture ammunition.

163
00:15:00,181 --> 00:15:08,320
Perhaps the Spencer earned its greatest fame on June 24, 1863, at the Battle of Hoover's Gap.

164
00:15:09,220 --> 00:15:16,700
Union Colonel John Thomas Wilder's Lightning Brigade suddenly found itself face to face with a Confederate force of superior numbers.

165
00:15:17,920 --> 00:15:21,680
For Wilder's men, the Spencers made all the difference.

166
00:15:23,660 --> 00:15:28,260
One of my regiments fairly defeated a rebel brigade of five regiments.

167
00:15:28,800 --> 00:15:33,580
They admitted a loss of over 500, while our loss was 47.

168
00:15:34,620 --> 00:15:37,320
My men feel as if it is impossible to be whipped,

169
00:15:37,720 --> 00:15:42,400
and the confidence inspired by these arms added to their terribly destructive capacity

170
00:15:42,401 --> 00:15:45,620
fully quadruples the effectiveness of my command.

171
00:15:46,360 --> 00:15:51,180
Colonel John Thomas Wilder, commanding the 1st Mounted Brigade of the 17th Indiana Volunteers.

172
00:15:51,181 --> 00:16:00,660
Hoover's Gap was the first battle in history in which repeating rifles gave troops a significant

173
00:16:00,661 --> 00:16:01,361
advantage.

174
00:16:01,362 --> 00:16:06,240
Despite this, the Union Ordnance Department in Washington, which had initially

175
00:16:06,241 --> 00:16:10,474
ordered 10,000 Spencers, refused to order any more.

176
00:16:10,475 --> 00:16:13,960
General Ripley, the chief of ordinance for

177
00:16:13,961 --> 00:16:19,720
the Union Army, felt that would be too much of a wasteful expenditure of ammunition on the part

178
00:16:19,721 --> 00:16:26,360
the Union marksman, and if they were able to fire 16 rounds in 10 seconds, then the cost of keeping

179
00:16:26,361 --> 00:16:30,815
the Union army in the field with ammunition up to that point would be astronomical.

180
00:16:30,816 --> 00:16:31,620
He could afford

181
00:16:31,621 --> 00:16:37,540
to keep an army in the field that fired three shots in one minute, but 16 shots in 10 seconds

182
00:16:37,541 --> 00:16:39,498
was an expensive proposition.

183
00:16:39,499 --> 00:16:43,480
He also felt that a soldier that was able to fire that fast

184
00:16:43,481 --> 00:16:49,020
wouldn't take such care in choosing his targets that there would be a lot of wasteful ammunition

185
00:16:49,021 --> 00:16:49,721
has been.

186
00:16:49,722 --> 00:16:55,720
But Christopher Spencer, then only 27 years old, refused to take no for an answer.

187
00:16:56,420 --> 00:17:02,000
He had other plans, namely the complete arming of all Union forces with his repeating rifle

188
00:17:02,001 --> 00:17:08,840
and the termination of Springfield musket production. Spencer bypassed Ripley and

189
00:17:08,841 --> 00:17:12,260
arranged a personal audience with President Lincoln to plead his case.

190
00:17:12,261 --> 00:17:21,040
on the 18th of august 1863 i arrived at the white house with the rifle in hand and was immediately

191
00:17:21,041 --> 00:17:27,620
ushered into the executive room i found the president alone with brief introduction i took

192
00:17:27,621 --> 00:17:33,580
the rifle from its case and presented it to him looking it over carefully and handling it as one

193
00:17:33,581 --> 00:17:38,400
familiar with firearms he requested me to take it apart to show the inwardness of the thing

194
00:17:38,401 --> 00:17:45,640
Lincoln was impressed and quickly made arrangements for a private demonstration with this new repeating weapon.

195
00:17:47,280 --> 00:17:54,600
Near where the Washington Memorial now stands, a Union officer set up a target as Lincoln and Spencer prepared to fire.

196
00:17:56,820 --> 00:17:59,080
The rifle contained seven cartridges.

197
00:17:59,940 --> 00:18:00,640
Mr.

198
00:18:00,641 --> 00:18:05,340
Lincoln's first shot was low, but the next shot hit the bullseye and the other five were close around it.

199
00:18:05,341 --> 00:18:08,840
Now, says he, we will let the inventor try it.

200
00:18:09,980 --> 00:18:13,580
Being in almost daily practice, I naturally beat the president a little.

201
00:18:14,260 --> 00:18:18,900
Well, he said, you are younger than I am, have a better eye, and a steadier nerve.

202
00:18:19,780 --> 00:18:22,000
Christopher Spencer, 1863.

203
00:18:23,500 --> 00:18:26,100
Spencer's demonstration had the desired effect.

204
00:18:26,860 --> 00:18:31,240
Lincoln ordered the War Department to supply as many troops as possible with Spencer rifles.

205
00:18:32,100 --> 00:18:37,940
By the end of the war, 144,000 were made and over 58 million cartridges ordered.

206
00:18:39,520 --> 00:18:41,380
The tide had changed.

207
00:18:41,860 --> 00:18:46,820
Within 20 years, repeating guns became the dominant weapon of all soldiers in land wars.

208
00:18:47,040 --> 00:18:54,540
At the same time, new advances in handguns were also assuring that a more deadly era of warfare had begun.

209
00:18:55,540 --> 00:18:59,700
The Spencer rifle was so instrumental to the Union's victory at Hoover's Gap

210
00:18:59,701 --> 00:19:02,260
that one member of the brigade wrote his wife,

211
00:19:02,620 --> 00:19:05,000
Our men adore them as the heathen do their idols.

212
00:19:05,460 --> 00:19:08,680
The guns of the Civil War will return on Modern Marvels.

213
00:19:10,160 --> 00:19:12,000
At the start of the Civil War,

214
00:19:12,300 --> 00:19:15,020
the U.S. Ordnance Department was reluctant to recommend

215
00:19:15,021 --> 00:19:17,960
the use of revolvers as a standard-issue firearm.

216
00:19:18,460 --> 00:19:21,900
But that soon changed as their practicality became apparent.

217
00:19:22,700 --> 00:19:26,560
Revolvers were reliable, close-range, multiple-firing weapons

218
00:19:26,561 --> 00:19:29,580
that were soon carried by almost every cavalryman,

219
00:19:29,700 --> 00:19:32,660
artillery crew, and officer on both sides.

220
00:19:33,760 --> 00:19:37,880
Though most revolvers used in the Civil War were muzzle-loading weapons,

221
00:19:38,360 --> 00:19:43,560
many had special innovations which even today stand out as technological marvels.

222
00:19:44,180 --> 00:19:48,560
One of the truly remarkable revolvers developed around the time of the Civil War

223
00:19:48,561 --> 00:19:53,440
was the Lamat, invented by Dr. Gene Alexander Lamat of New Orleans.

224
00:19:53,441 --> 00:20:04,800
One of its unique features was that the center pin was actually hollowed, so it took a 16-gauge shotgun buckshot,

225
00:20:05,160 --> 00:20:12,500
and the revolver barrel itself had in the cylinder nine shots in .42 caliber.

226
00:20:13,040 --> 00:20:18,240
It was a muzzleloader, so here is the loading lever on the side of the barrel.

227
00:20:18,241 --> 00:20:26,640
In order to fire the center barrel, you pushed down an aperture on the hammer, and a nipple went here.

228
00:20:27,260 --> 00:20:35,900
In order to fire the revolver cylinder, you switched the firing aperture back up on the hammer, and you could fire those nine shots.

229
00:20:36,940 --> 00:20:43,180
A formidable weapon using a LeMatte in combat was like having a revolver and a shotgun all in one.

230
00:20:43,181 --> 00:20:49,480
I can't imagine what a handheld 16-gauge shotgun would be like,

231
00:20:50,040 --> 00:20:52,220
but I would certainly think it would get your attention.

232
00:20:52,560 --> 00:20:55,880
It would obviously be devastating at close range.

233
00:20:56,940 --> 00:21:00,360
3,000 lemats were used by the Confederacy during the war.

234
00:21:01,120 --> 00:21:05,020
Among its patrons was the famous rebel cavalry general, Jeb Stewart.

235
00:21:06,500 --> 00:21:11,160
Even though it was an expensive weapon to own during the war, costing almost $50,

236
00:21:11,161 --> 00:21:18,160
dollars, a LeMatte in good condition now is worth over fifteen thousand dollars.

237
00:21:18,161 --> 00:21:23,080
Though LeMatte's employed several extraordinary design features, the most

238
00:21:23,081 --> 00:21:26,520
popular handgun of the war was manufactured by a charismatic

239
00:21:26,521 --> 00:21:32,520
entrepreneur from Connecticut named Samuel Colt. Samuel Colt in 1836 is

240
00:21:32,521 --> 00:21:37,740
credited with being the father of the revolver, a handheld gun that was able

241
00:21:37,741 --> 00:21:43,120
to discharge multiple rounds with successive working of the action of the gun.

242
00:21:43,680 --> 00:21:47,060
The revolving gun replaced the standard pistol of the period,

243
00:21:47,220 --> 00:21:52,100
which was like the rifles of the period, a single-shot muzzle-loading implement.

244
00:21:54,080 --> 00:21:58,100
Colt's genius as an inventor was to manufacture a handheld weapon

245
00:21:58,101 --> 00:22:00,460
that would stand up to the rigors of warfare.

246
00:22:01,460 --> 00:22:07,840
The Colt Army Revolver Model 1860 was a simple, rugged, and reliable single-action handgun.

247
00:22:08,940 --> 00:22:15,540
It had an improved loading lever system over previous models called a rack and pinion.

248
00:22:17,160 --> 00:22:25,240
It had standard an 8-inch barrel, which was perfect ballistically for the best accuracy.

249
00:22:25,241 --> 00:22:34,620
It was a six-shooter, and it was made of the finest steel, and was as modern as Colt had ever made any handgun.

250
00:22:35,360 --> 00:22:41,280
The 1860 Army Colt was the most popular and widely distributed revolver of the Civil War.

251
00:22:41,900 --> 00:22:47,320
Approximately 200,000 were made, and a great many of those were issued to Union troops.

252
00:22:48,140 --> 00:22:53,660
But Sam Colt wasn't just one of the greatest arms inventors and manufacturers of the 19th century.

253
00:22:53,661 --> 00:22:56,400
He was also a shrewd businessman.

254
00:22:57,240 --> 00:23:02,220
The advertising of the day said the Colt revolver was the world's right arm.

255
00:23:02,760 --> 00:23:08,460
Others said that if God hadn't created men equal, Samuel Colt had made them equal.

256
00:23:09,700 --> 00:23:14,740
There are still extant, beautiful case sets of presentation Colt revolvers

257
00:23:14,741 --> 00:23:18,880
given to secretaries of war, chief of the ordnance department,

258
00:23:18,881 --> 00:23:27,680
general officers of any importance, and also minor ordnance officers that might sit on boards that

259
00:23:27,681 --> 00:23:29,256
would select weapons.

260
00:23:29,257 --> 00:23:35,260
So Colt was really, he was a businessman first, and possibly an inventor second.

261
00:23:38,380 --> 00:23:42,631
Today, a Colt 1860 revolver can be bought for $1,500.

262
00:23:42,632 --> 00:23:45,840
A specially engraved model is worth many

263
00:23:45,841 --> 00:23:47,105
times more than that.

264
00:23:47,106 --> 00:23:51,260
But Colt's ambition went beyond simply manufacturing the most popular

265
00:23:51,261 --> 00:23:52,471
handgun of the war.

266
00:23:52,472 --> 00:23:57,700
The beginning of the war, Sam Colt, who had always billed himself as Colonel Colt,

267
00:23:58,280 --> 00:24:02,631
had tried to raise a regiment to take into the field at the outset of the war.

268
00:24:02,632 --> 00:24:03,332
And in fact,

269
00:24:03,380 --> 00:24:07,660
it offered his services to the United States government and then to the state of Connecticut,

270
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:15,720
his home state, to allow him to personally, at his expense, arm and equip uniform on entire

271
00:24:15,721 --> 00:24:19,304
regiment of men carrying Colt revolving rifles.

272
00:24:19,305 --> 00:24:22,660
In fact, those offers were turned down. Colt

273
00:24:22,661 --> 00:24:25,900
never did take the field as a military officer.

274
00:24:27,800 --> 00:24:33,500
However, Sam Colt did rule the wartime revolver industry because of his ability to manufacture

275
00:24:33,501 --> 00:24:39,120
mass quantities of guns. He may also have damaged his reputation by overcharging for

276
00:24:39,121 --> 00:24:39,821
them.

277
00:24:39,822 --> 00:24:45,700
He priced his guns at $25 a piece when it only cost him half as much to produce war.

278
00:24:45,720 --> 00:24:52,540
An enterprising gunmaker from New York State saw this greediness on Colt's part as an opportunity for himself.

279
00:24:53,440 --> 00:24:54,480
Eli Remington, Jr.

280
00:24:54,481 --> 00:24:58,640
made a revolver that was actually preferred by combat-hardened officers.

281
00:25:00,680 --> 00:25:10,980
Remington produced a solid-frame revolver, which was very strong, very reliable, not subject to breakage as much as Colt's.

282
00:25:11,840 --> 00:25:15,400
But Remington didn't have the public relations department that Colt did.

283
00:25:15,720 --> 00:25:23,720
Remington was only able to convince the Ordnance Department to purchase 5,000 of his new Army Model 44 revolvers.

284
00:25:23,721 --> 00:25:33,720
Then in 1862, Remington testified in the Ordnance Commission hearings that he could manufacture his revolvers for as little as $12 apiece.

285
00:25:33,721 --> 00:25:40,720
In addition, he said he could produce any other design, including Colts, for the same low price.

286
00:25:40,721 --> 00:25:48,100
Remington and his sons who were in business with him offered a very reliable firearm.

287
00:25:48,101 --> 00:25:56,180
They weren't in for profiteering as there had been charges that had been leveled at other manufacturers such as Colt.

288
00:25:57,360 --> 00:26:03,640
The Colt Company's $25 a gun bonanza was at an end and Remington was filling the gap.

289
00:26:03,641 --> 00:26:12,160
By 1865, Remington had supplied 125,000 revolvers to the U.S. Army,

290
00:26:12,620 --> 00:26:17,800
and Colt revolvers were selling for the reduced price of $14.50 a gun.

291
00:26:18,420 --> 00:26:24,420
The rapid development in the design of small arms during the Civil War was nothing short of revolutionary.

292
00:26:25,460 --> 00:26:29,020
And as the weapons changed, so too did the nature of war.

293
00:26:29,780 --> 00:26:32,660
The conflict was being played out on a grand scale,

294
00:26:32,661 --> 00:26:36,860
a scale which demanded even larger and more destructive weapons.

295
00:26:38,760 --> 00:26:43,160
At the Battle of Chancellorsville, Stonewall Jackson was killed by friendly fire.

296
00:26:43,600 --> 00:26:48,100
His party was mistaken for a Union cavalry force by Confederate troops and fired upon.

297
00:26:48,720 --> 00:26:50,260
Jackson was hit by three bullets.

298
00:26:50,540 --> 00:26:53,900
His arm had to be amputated, and he died eight days later of pneumonia.

299
00:26:54,600 --> 00:26:57,740
The guns of the Civil War will continue on Modern Marvels.

300
00:26:57,741 --> 00:27:05,440
As with repeaters and revolvers, light artillery in the Civil War was making significant advances.

301
00:27:07,480 --> 00:27:12,420
These new weapons of death were astounding in their ability to kill on a massive scale.

302
00:27:15,120 --> 00:27:21,720
Used by both sides, the real workhorse of the Civil War was a piece of light artillery called the Napoleon.

303
00:27:21,721 --> 00:27:30,540
Named after Emperor Napoleon III of France, this brass smoothbore could fire a projectile over 1,600 yards.

304
00:27:31,200 --> 00:27:35,240
But its deadly effectiveness was most apparent at much closer range.

305
00:27:36,620 --> 00:27:40,460
It was loaded with a piece of ammunition called canister.

306
00:27:40,860 --> 00:27:46,040
And that was essentially a can with about 27 smaller round balls in it.

307
00:27:47,160 --> 00:27:50,560
And when that was fired, just like a shotgun, it exploded.

308
00:27:50,561 --> 00:27:53,980
the shot dispersed over a fairly large area,

309
00:27:54,280 --> 00:27:58,120
especially if you were an attacking army at 300 yards away.

310
00:27:58,540 --> 00:28:01,560
By the time the canister got to you, it was very, very large,

311
00:28:01,680 --> 00:28:03,920
very wide, and very destructive.

312
00:28:04,540 --> 00:28:08,580
There were accounts in the Civil War of soldiers charging up to a Napoleon gun

313
00:28:08,581 --> 00:28:11,440
that goes off and just becoming vaporized.

314
00:28:11,880 --> 00:28:13,600
It was a very, very effective weapon,

315
00:28:13,680 --> 00:28:15,560
and this is one of the reasons why tactics change.

316
00:28:17,020 --> 00:28:19,720
The effect of a Napoleon was devastating.

317
00:28:20,340 --> 00:28:22,200
Like gigantic shotguns,

318
00:28:22,300 --> 00:28:25,120
Napoleons loaded with canisters caused more casualties

319
00:28:25,121 --> 00:28:28,320
than all other artillery projectiles combined.

320
00:28:29,320 --> 00:28:31,340
And yet in battle after battle,

321
00:28:31,700 --> 00:28:35,320
rows of men lined up and charged these mounted artillery pieces.

322
00:28:35,900 --> 00:28:37,540
Most would be butchered.

323
00:28:38,480 --> 00:28:43,020
The Civil War soldier usually was part of a unit

324
00:28:43,021 --> 00:28:47,820
that was raised from his hometown or his home county.

325
00:28:47,821 --> 00:28:52,900
everybody in his company was a personal friend or an acquaintance.

326
00:28:53,940 --> 00:28:58,420
There were strong family bonds, intermarriages between families.

327
00:28:58,940 --> 00:29:04,940
These soldiers refused to run because they knew that they were going to have to go back to their communities

328
00:29:04,941 --> 00:29:09,200
after the war if they survived and live with these people.

329
00:29:09,201 --> 00:29:20,100
They had a common spirit decor, a common hope of survival, a common spirituality, if you will.

330
00:29:20,520 --> 00:29:27,140
But they all believed they had to show that they could do their best to their neighbors.

331
00:29:27,760 --> 00:29:34,680
And they would be disgraced, absolutely disgraced, if they showed the white feather and ran.

332
00:29:34,681 --> 00:29:48,681
And I think this is one of the primary reasons that seasoned soldiers, knowing full well what double canister would do to their ranks, would advance without hesitation

333
00:29:48,712 --> 00:29:50,560
into obvious oblivion.

334
00:29:50,920 --> 00:29:58,060
They knew they were going to die, but they preferred an honorable death over what they perceived as cowardice.

335
00:29:58,061 --> 00:30:06,260
As the land war progressed and the death toll mounted, a new form of warfare was taking place on the seas.

336
00:30:07,100 --> 00:30:12,860
Navies on both sides were quickly learning that wooden ships of war were increasingly vulnerable to artillery,

337
00:30:13,460 --> 00:30:17,360
which, thanks in part to rifling, was becoming larger and more accurate.

338
00:30:18,820 --> 00:30:24,400
The Union and Confederacy began retrofitting the exterior of their ships with thick sheets of iron,

339
00:30:25,080 --> 00:30:27,760
solid protection against incoming artillery shells,

340
00:30:28,060 --> 00:30:32,920
The iron sheets were slanted, causing the projectiles to expend their force upward.

341
00:30:33,800 --> 00:30:36,060
They would ricochet off and away from the ship.

342
00:30:37,980 --> 00:30:43,000
A brilliant Swedish inventor named John Erickson saw these new ironclads being built

343
00:30:43,001 --> 00:30:47,560
and was hard at work on an invention that would revolutionize naval warfare.

344
00:30:47,561 --> 00:31:00,840
the federals to combat the confederate ironclads developed a ship that had a unique capability

345
00:31:00,841 --> 00:31:10,360
of a revolving turret this was called the cheese box on a raft and john erickson developed it

346
00:31:10,361 --> 00:31:15,280
and it actually, from the beginning until it hit the water,

347
00:31:15,500 --> 00:31:18,160
was 100 days construction time.

348
00:31:19,500 --> 00:31:24,140
The USS Monitor was completed on January 30, 1862

349
00:31:24,141 --> 00:31:27,420
and was one of the strangest sights ever seen in the water.

350
00:31:28,140 --> 00:31:31,140
The ship's deck was only 14 inches above the waterline.

351
00:31:31,700 --> 00:31:33,300
Waves splashed on her surface.

352
00:31:33,720 --> 00:31:36,100
With so little exterior to protect,

353
00:31:36,360 --> 00:31:38,760
the ship's armor could be focused on her gun turret.

354
00:31:40,360 --> 00:31:48,380
Inside that turret were two 11-inch smoothbore Dahlgrens, which shot a solid projectile weighing 180 pounds.

355
00:31:49,280 --> 00:31:51,800
Firing could take place once every two minutes.

356
00:31:52,720 --> 00:31:56,460
It had a steam-driven turret. It only mounted two guns.

357
00:31:57,580 --> 00:32:01,640
These guns were protected inside this revolving turret.

358
00:32:02,320 --> 00:32:09,420
They could be withdrawn, and the portholes shuttered, so the guns could be reloaded in relative safety.

359
00:32:09,421 --> 00:32:14,600
and then the guns run out and fired at the Confederate antagonist.

360
00:32:15,240 --> 00:32:18,580
The Monitor engaged the Confederate ironclad Virginia,

361
00:32:19,000 --> 00:32:20,580
otherwise known as the Merrimack,

362
00:32:20,760 --> 00:32:24,220
at Hampton Roads, Virginia on March 9, 1862.

363
00:32:24,800 --> 00:32:27,840
It was one of the most important naval battles in history,

364
00:32:28,300 --> 00:32:30,260
marking the end of the wooden navy

365
00:32:30,261 --> 00:32:32,720
and the beginning of the armored cruiser.

366
00:32:33,880 --> 00:32:36,300
Though the battle lasted only two hours,

367
00:32:36,520 --> 00:32:38,280
the world saw for the first time

368
00:32:38,281 --> 00:32:41,500
the deadly value of a rotating gun in naval combat.

369
00:32:43,160 --> 00:32:49,300
The ironclads of the Confederates had their guns mounted in broadside primarily,

370
00:32:49,540 --> 00:32:55,420
also a stern gun, but these were the configuration of the old Navy.

371
00:32:56,100 --> 00:32:59,780
The whole ship had to be maneuvered so that the broadside could fire.

372
00:32:59,980 --> 00:33:05,100
The monitor, on the other hand, could swivel the turret,

373
00:33:05,101 --> 00:33:10,060
could turn its turret almost 360 degrees and fire in any direction.

374
00:33:10,420 --> 00:33:13,200
So the ship itself did not have to be maneuvered.

375
00:33:13,220 --> 00:33:14,360
The turret could be turned.

376
00:33:15,140 --> 00:33:18,120
It was a design concept for battleship guns

377
00:33:18,121 --> 00:33:20,420
which remained seminal through the 20th century.

378
00:33:21,240 --> 00:33:25,200
But conditions inside the turret were something less than advanced.

379
00:33:26,320 --> 00:33:31,860
The turrets of the Monitor class were made of armor plate.

380
00:33:31,861 --> 00:33:37,860
They did have grates, open grates on the top so heat could escape.

381
00:33:37,861 --> 00:33:45,860
But the interior of the turret was cramped, it was dark, it was incredibly smoky.

382
00:33:45,861 --> 00:33:53,860
You have to try and imagine what it must have felt like for the sailors manning the guns

383
00:33:53,861 --> 00:34:01,500
to hear 100 and 200-pound projectiles hitting the outside of the turret,

384
00:34:02,220 --> 00:34:09,460
a continuous din, and it's not really a surprise that many sailors who served on ironclads

385
00:34:09,461 --> 00:34:17,840
and applied for pensions after the war complained of total 100% deafness from ruptured eardrums

386
00:34:17,841 --> 00:34:21,920
due to the concussion sustained working guns and ironclad turrets.

387
00:34:23,860 --> 00:34:31,560
The type of innovative thinking exhibited in shipbuilding was also being applied to the science of rapid-fire artillery.

388
00:34:31,561 --> 00:34:40,660
In an effort to give the soldier even more firepower, an unusual class of weapons known as volley guns were being experimented with in the field.

389
00:34:42,940 --> 00:34:50,640
Volley guns are multi-shot devices that closely resembled weapons projected by Leonardo da Vinci and date back to the 16th century.

390
00:34:51,640 --> 00:34:56,260
The Vandenberg Volley Gun had anywhere from 85 to 451 barrels.

391
00:34:56,780 --> 00:34:59,660
They could be fired individually or all at once.

392
00:35:00,840 --> 00:35:03,400
Another was the Billinghurst Requiegun.

393
00:35:04,180 --> 00:35:07,760
Requiring a crew of three, the gun was usually mounted on wheels

394
00:35:07,761 --> 00:35:10,280
and could be fired up to seven times in a minute.

395
00:35:11,080 --> 00:35:16,620
It was a collection of barrels that were fired by one percussion cap,

396
00:35:16,621 --> 00:35:22,040
And as the first cartridges went off, the flames spread from cartridge to cartridge,

397
00:35:22,180 --> 00:35:22,980
and they all went off.

398
00:35:23,640 --> 00:35:24,860
Bam, bam, bam, bam, bam.

399
00:35:25,980 --> 00:35:28,200
With the introduction of each new weapon,

400
00:35:28,500 --> 00:35:32,720
it was becoming apparent that Napoleonic tactics were completely outmoded.

401
00:35:33,520 --> 00:35:36,360
Based on weapons technology from earlier in the century,

402
00:35:36,940 --> 00:35:39,860
romantic notions of battle had less and less significance.

403
00:35:40,660 --> 00:35:46,040
Those tactics were becoming supplanted by the cold, hard mechanization of the Civil War.

404
00:35:46,620 --> 00:35:54,860
Soon, one of the last great battles of the war would be fought, and it would help shape

405
00:35:54,861 --> 00:36:01,400
battle tactics well into the next century.

406
00:36:01,401 --> 00:36:07,300
By 1864, the overwhelming strength of the North was bearing down hard on the Confederacy,

407
00:36:07,301 --> 00:36:12,240
and new types of heavy guns were forever changing the way wars were to be fought.

408
00:36:12,241 --> 00:36:17,440
The Siege of Charleston saw the Union Army employ some of the biggest guns in use anywhere in the world.

409
00:36:19,720 --> 00:36:23,360
At the Siege of Charleston, which was a long, protracted affair,

410
00:36:23,860 --> 00:36:29,100
the Union Army, desperate to capture this place where what they would have called the rebellion, began,

411
00:36:29,900 --> 00:36:35,220
you see a variety of heavy ordnance brought to bear, not just on the defenses of Charleston,

412
00:36:35,221 --> 00:36:38,520
not just on the military fortifications, but on the town itself.

413
00:36:38,521 --> 00:36:45,740
You even see one huge gun that was nicknamed the Swamp Angel erected on an artificial island,

414
00:36:46,300 --> 00:36:49,940
basically built out of sandbags and earth in the middle of a swamp,

415
00:36:50,480 --> 00:36:57,340
that can lob a shell several miles into Charleston so that the people of the city,

416
00:36:58,120 --> 00:37:03,400
literally going about their business, would have scant warning before the shriek and the explosion of this shell.

417
00:37:04,420 --> 00:37:06,500
Other guns were employed as well.

418
00:37:06,501 --> 00:37:14,200
The Rodman weighed 117,000 pounds and could fire a half-ton projectile 8,000 yards.

419
00:37:16,360 --> 00:37:24,020
And the Whitworth could blast an 80-pound projectile almost 14,000 yards, a distance of more than 7 miles.

420
00:37:26,240 --> 00:37:32,700
But the big guns that would essentially just lob a shell for however many miles into the enemy position,

421
00:37:33,140 --> 00:37:35,280
you're really not sure what you're going to be hitting with that.

422
00:37:35,281 --> 00:37:37,820
And that is a very modern way of waging war.

423
00:37:37,980 --> 00:37:40,340
It's not the gentleman's war.

424
00:37:40,520 --> 00:37:44,080
It's not the Napoleonic way of waging war.

425
00:37:44,160 --> 00:37:45,440
It's not war as a glory.

426
00:37:46,780 --> 00:37:50,640
And certainly you see more of that as the Civil War goes on.

427
00:37:51,940 --> 00:37:58,240
While they cause no military damage, the effect on civilian morale was devastating

428
00:37:58,241 --> 00:38:05,620
because the city knew that they were under fire at any given time

429
00:38:05,621 --> 00:38:08,920
at the will of the Union blockading forces.

430
00:38:10,300 --> 00:38:14,540
Anxious to take the Confederate capital, Union troops in 1864

431
00:38:14,541 --> 00:38:18,840
laid siege to Richmond's last line of defense, Petersburg.

432
00:38:19,880 --> 00:38:22,420
In this final great battle of the Civil War,

433
00:38:22,421 --> 00:38:27,100
the army of Ulysses S. Grant faced off directly against Robert E. Lee's forces.

434
00:38:28,240 --> 00:38:33,860
By now, leaders on both sides had come to appreciate the advances made by guns and artillery.

435
00:38:34,740 --> 00:38:39,940
In place of thousands of men being sent to their deaths in futile charges against advanced weaponry,

436
00:38:40,540 --> 00:38:43,020
soldiers were instead hunkering down in trenches.

437
00:38:45,400 --> 00:38:50,200
Petersburg certainly, one cannot look at those images of the trenches and the defenses

438
00:38:50,201 --> 00:38:55,420
in the muddy, corpse-strewn battlefields there

439
00:38:55,421 --> 00:38:58,540
and not think of the First World War.

440
00:38:59,340 --> 00:39:03,460
Certainly, Petersburg, because of that visual record we have,

441
00:39:03,461 --> 00:39:07,800
is one of the more dramatic and obvious examples of the Civil War

442
00:39:07,801 --> 00:39:11,740
hearkening forward to a more modern era of warfare.

443
00:39:12,960 --> 00:39:15,980
Foreshadowing the need for mobile artillery in later wars,

444
00:39:16,420 --> 00:39:19,800
the Union Army placed a 13-inch mortar on a railway carriage

445
00:39:19,801 --> 00:39:23,000
and used it to shell Confederate forces protecting Petersburg.

446
00:39:24,980 --> 00:39:27,580
Soldiers would position the carriage on a curved track

447
00:39:27,581 --> 00:39:30,140
and aim the gun by moving it back and forth.

448
00:39:33,700 --> 00:39:37,400
Both sides had also developed several primitive machine guns.

449
00:39:38,200 --> 00:39:40,700
The Ager machine gun, nicknamed the coffee mill,

450
00:39:41,020 --> 00:39:43,380
could fire up to 120 rounds a minute.

451
00:39:44,040 --> 00:39:47,060
The rate was kept low for fear of overheating the barrel.

452
00:39:47,061 --> 00:39:56,480
The Union coffee mill gun gets its name from the fact that it had a hopper on top that looked like a coffee mill.

453
00:39:57,040 --> 00:40:04,640
And these preloaded cartridges were dropped into the hopper and the crank fed them into the mechanism of the gun.

454
00:40:05,580 --> 00:40:12,120
The Williams machine gun was supposed to be the Confederate's secret weapon, and the Confederacy ordered 42 of them.

455
00:40:12,121 --> 00:40:20,820
It fired 65 rounds per minute, but extended firing overheated the barrel, a casualty of the poor quality of Confederate steel.

456
00:40:21,520 --> 00:40:28,760
But the siege of Petersburg saw the first use of another rapid-fire gun that became the forerunner of the modern machine gun.

457
00:40:31,260 --> 00:40:34,330
Known as the Gatling gun, its inventor Richard J.

458
00:40:34,331 --> 00:40:38,840
Gatling of North Carolina took out the first patents on his gun in 1862.

459
00:40:38,841 --> 00:40:42,820
It was an ingenious invention, though it was far from perfect.

460
00:40:43,420 --> 00:40:48,820
The gun tended to jam, it was heavy, and sustained firing produced a great deal of smoke.

461
00:40:49,680 --> 00:40:54,920
But the use of six rotating barrels was a clever solution to the difficulty of overheating,

462
00:40:55,480 --> 00:41:00,240
and a Gatling was capable of firing between 200 and 400 rounds a minute.

463
00:41:01,080 --> 00:41:06,040
In a letter he wrote to Samuel Colt's widow following the Civil War,

464
00:41:06,041 --> 00:41:06,741
Dr.

465
00:41:06,742 --> 00:41:12,600
Gatling wrote how his inspiration for the development of the Gatling gun had come about.

466
00:41:13,220 --> 00:41:20,440
And he had witnessed troops returning from the front at a local train station near his home in the Midwest

467
00:41:20,441 --> 00:41:25,060
and had seen the ravages of the wounds that these soldiers were carrying,

468
00:41:25,540 --> 00:41:30,040
tremendous damage and suffering that these poor guys were undergoing

469
00:41:30,041 --> 00:41:33,960
as they were being returned to their homes to convalesce.

470
00:41:33,961 --> 00:41:40,240
it struck him that if he could develop a gun that was so terrible

471
00:41:40,241 --> 00:41:43,520
and the amount of firepower it could lay down,

472
00:41:44,020 --> 00:41:48,300
that one army equipped with a number of these guns

473
00:41:48,301 --> 00:41:54,860
would intimidate any other army into just realizing the futility of attacking

474
00:41:54,861 --> 00:41:57,860
and cease to remain in the field.

475
00:41:58,320 --> 00:42:02,940
Ironically, in subsequent years, Gatling's invention would have the opposite effect.

476
00:42:02,941 --> 00:42:05,980
It made battlefields many times more deadly.

477
00:42:06,740 --> 00:42:11,380
But during the Civil War, his attempts to sell this gun to the Ordnance Department were rejected.

478
00:42:12,500 --> 00:42:16,000
However, Union General Benjamin Butler was convinced of their effectiveness,

479
00:42:16,600 --> 00:42:20,580
and with $1,000 of his own money, privately purchased 12 Gatleins.

480
00:42:21,100 --> 00:42:25,440
In 1865, he had his troops employ them during the Siege of Petersburg.

481
00:42:26,160 --> 00:42:31,900
General Benjamin Butler was one of the more unusual Civil War generals.

482
00:42:32,940 --> 00:42:36,700
in that he was a politician, he was a governor,

483
00:42:37,580 --> 00:42:41,680
he had a little military training, but he was a brilliant man.

484
00:42:42,360 --> 00:42:47,660
Butler bought weapons that he considered to be of some use to him,

485
00:42:47,661 --> 00:42:52,940
and one of the weapons that he bought was a battery of Gatling guns.

486
00:42:53,820 --> 00:42:57,680
Despite Butler's purchase, Colonel Ripley of the Union Ordnance Office

487
00:42:57,681 --> 00:43:00,460
refused to consider the need for a Gatling gun.

488
00:43:00,461 --> 00:43:07,460
The irony is that the North could have produced a reliable machine gun, like the Gatling,

489
00:43:07,461 --> 00:43:11,460
in sufficient quantities to have significantly influenced the outcome of the war.

490
00:43:11,461 --> 00:43:19,460
But men of limited vision chose to ignore a concept that would soon become a standard feature of warfare throughout the world.

491
00:43:19,461 --> 00:43:25,460
The Civil War was a preview of the shape of things to come,

492
00:43:25,461 --> 00:43:28,460
and marked an important transition in the science of guns.

493
00:43:28,461 --> 00:43:35,360
technology had moved these machines of war far beyond those found in Napoleon's time and thrust

494
00:43:35,361 --> 00:43:42,320
the tactics of warfare well into the future some 50 years later when war breaks out on the Western

495
00:43:42,321 --> 00:43:48,140
front of Europe things seem to pick up technologically or tactically where they left off

496
00:43:48,141 --> 00:43:55,460
at Petersburg in 1865 no longer could spree decor of the unit carry the battlefield you had to have

497
00:43:55,461 --> 00:44:01,100
superior firepower to reach the enemy's lines and against a firm entrenched

498
00:44:01,101 --> 00:44:07,100
position that was near impossibility. More Americans were killed in the Civil

499
00:44:07,101 --> 00:44:11,540
War than all other American wars combined.

500
00:44:11,541 --> 00:44:15,860
It was the first modern war and foreshadowed the horrors to come in the

501
00:44:15,861 --> 00:44:21,300
beginning of the next century. But any war, especially one as brutal as the

502
00:44:21,301 --> 00:44:26,980
American Civil War proves that technological innovation can contribute to victory, but

503
00:44:26,981 --> 00:44:29,860
only at a horrible cost of flesh and blood.

